<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The panel's fill. 22dp corners, solid white, no stroke.

    ## Why white, and why this is an ImageView src rather than a background

    The fill is a *setting*: the setup screen writes a ground (Surface / Ink /
    Accent / None) and an opacity 0-100 into WidgetConfigStore, and the
    provider composes them onto this shape at build time.

    The obvious way to apply it — `setInt(panel, "setBackgroundColor", argb)` —
    replaces the view's background outright, which would take the 22dp corners
    and the 1dp stroke with it and leave a square slab. So instead this shape
    is the `src` of an ImageView and the provider applies:

        setInt(id, "setColorFilter", groundRgb)   // SRC_ATOP over white
        setInt(id, "setImageAlpha", 0..255)       // the opacity slider

    Both are @RemotableViewMethod on ImageView and have been since API 16, so
    this needs no API gate (minSdk is 24). Filtering *white* is what makes the
    ground come through exactly as specified rather than blended with a base
    colour, and the transparent corners stay transparent because SRC_ATOP
    leaves destination alpha alone.

    That is also why the stroke is NOT here: it lives in widget_panel_edge.xml
    on its own view, so it keeps full opacity while the fill fades. "Only the
    panel fades — text, the accent bar and the icons keep their own opaque
    colors" applies to the edge too; a 1px rim that dissolves with the slider
    is how a panel stops having a shape.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <corners android:radius="22dp" />
    <solid android:color="#FFFFFFFF" />
</shape>
